when you eat food there's much more to  food than just the taste in addition to  the taste the texture the way the food  feels in your mouth as a good example  the steak that we have here we're going  to cook the steak and I'm going to show  you how it feels how the way it feels  changes when you cook it what that means  is when you chew on the steak how it  feels when you chew on it changes that's  the texture that's the mouthfeel and  that's determined by the elasticity of  the steak the elasticity of the steak  will increase as it becomes more  difficult to chew as we cook it for a  longer period of time so all foods have  a texture all foods have a mouthfeel if  you don't like steak for example if you  prefer tofu for your vegetarian it too  comes in different flavors it comes in  different styles you can get a firm tofu  or you can get a soft tofu a firm tofu  takes more force from your mouth to chew  it it's more elastic it's stiffer soft  tofu is much softer it's much easier to  chew and when you finish the steak and  your tofu you have dessert you have  chocolate it too comes with different  textures chocolate mousse it's light its  Airy it's very easy to chew it's a very  low elastic material the elasticity of  chocolate mousse is very low if you take  a chocolate bar it's stiffer it takes  more force to chew it its elasticity  increases and if you take real chunk  chocolate a really good Belgian  chocolate it's much stiffer you have to  bite much harder so the mouthfeel how it  tastes all is wrapped up in the  elasticity of a food and this is how we  characterize what the mouthfeel of the  food is so if we want to measure it we  have to measure the elasticity and let's  start with the very simplest thing let's  start with a spring here I have a spring  and I can hang a weight on the end of  the spring if I hang a weight on the end  of this  the spring lengthens it becomes longer  it becomes longer by a certain amount  that mount that Delta L the change in  length is determined by how stiff by how  elastic the spring is and how large the  weight is so let me hang the weight on  it the spring gets longer but then it  stops moving the forces are balanced  there's no longer a net force on the  spring the spring is exerting a force to  hold the weight up and the weight is  exerting a force to stretch the spring  we can do the same thing if instead of  lengthening the spring we compress the  spring we put the weight on top of the  spring like this if you push on the  spring and you push it down then the  spring pushes upwards it pushes in the  opposite direction it tries to bring  itself back to its original length and  we can write the relationship between  the force of the spring and the change  in the length of the spring it's called  Hookes law and it just says that the  force is linearly proportional to the  change in length this minus sign arises  because there is always a restoring  force the string spring is always trying  to retain its original length it's  trying to get longer if you're  compressing it and it's trying to get  shorter if you're extending it the  relationship between the change in  length and the force is the spring  constant and that determines the  elasticity of the spring the larger the  spring constant is the more the force it  takes to extend it by a given length so  a stiff spring has a very large spring  constant and it takes a larger force to  extend it we can use the same concept  now to describe food in the case of food  we have some object for example we have  this piece of steak we want to measure  the elasticity of a piece of steak the  force that we put on the food the force  that we put on the steak will depend on  the size of the state  so if I take a very large piece of steak  and I want to deform it I want to  squeeze it I want to change its  thickness by a certain amount it takes a  much larger force than if I take a small  piece of steak but when I tell you what  the elasticity of the steak is I don't  want to have the dimensions of the  object of the piece of steak as part of  the elasticity so I want to somehow take  out the dimensions of the state so to do  that I take the force and I normalize  the force by the area on which I apply  the force that gives me the stress the  same point I don't want to know how  thick the steak is when I compress it I  just want to know how much I compress it  for a given thickness so I take the  compression I can't take the change in  thickness as I squeeze on the steak and  I normalize it by the original length  and that gives me a strain a strain is  the relative change in length now I can  calculate the elastic constant in the  same way that I calculate the spring  constant I want to calculate the spring  constant I take a force and it divided  by a change in length for the elastic  constant of the steak I take the stress  which is the force divided by the area  and I divide that by the strain which is  the relative change in length and that  gives me the elastic constant of the  food
 

